WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.264
The last problem of this lecture

00:00:03.264 --> 00:00:04.880
maximum subarray.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.023
The input is an array of integers.

00:00:08.023 --> 00:00:10.538
The output is a contiguous

00:00:10.538 --> 00:00:14.220
non-empty subarray with the largest sum.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.618
If all the numbers are positive, as here

00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:19.477
the whole array is best

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:21.930
its sum is 118.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.188
With some signs flipped

00:00:24.188 --> 00:00:25.858
it gets interesting.

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:28.641
The best is 17, -5

00:00:28.641 --> 00:00:30.629
28: sum 40.

00:00:30.709 --> 00:00:33.094
Taking the -5 is worth

00:00:33.174 --> 00:00:35.480
it to reach the 28.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.343
Taking the -21 is not

00:00:38.422 --> 00:00:40.490
the 18 after it does

00:00:40.569 --> 00:00:42.080
not make up for it.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.167
And if all the numbers are negative

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:48.209
the best is the single largest element

00:00:48.209 --> 00:00:49.198
-3.

00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:52.467
A classic use: the numbers are daily price

00:00:52.543 --> 00:00:53.988
changes of a stock

00:00:54.064 --> 00:00:56.117
and the answer says when to

00:00:56.193 --> 00:00:57.790
buy and when to sell.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:02.504
The obvious algorithm tries every subarray.

00:01:02.504 --> 00:01:06.349
There are n * (n + 1) / 2

00:01:06.349 --> 00:01:07.503
pairs i, j.

00:01:07.503 --> 00:01:11.733
Adding up each one takes up to n additions

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:15.290
so three nested loops: O(n³).

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.023
Better: precompute prefix sums.

00:01:19.023 --> 00:01:23.716
R[i] is the sum of the first i elements.

00:01:23.825 --> 00:01:26.990
Then the sum from i to j is

00:01:27.099 --> 00:01:30.154
R[j] - R[i - 1]

00:01:30.154 --> 00:01:32.010
one subtraction.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.720
Two nested loops: O(n²).

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.069
And no algorithm can beat linear time

00:01:39.069 --> 00:01:41.990
since it must look at every element.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.026
Here is the prefix-sum version in Java.

00:01:46.026 --> 00:01:48.418
Java arrays start at zero

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:51.165
so r[i] is the sum of a[0]

00:01:51.254 --> 00:01:53.292
up to a[i - 1]

00:01:53.292 --> 00:01:55.330
and r[0] is zero.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.285
Then two loops over all pairs i and j

00:02:00.406 --> 00:02:03.557
and the sum of a[i..j]

00:02:03.678 --> 00:02:07.920
is r[j + 1] - r[i].

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.612
Look at where the inner loop starts

00:02:11.612 --> 00:02:13.190
at j = i.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:14.729
That matters.

00:02:14.803 --> 00:02:16.941
Subarrays of a single element

00:02:17.015 --> 00:02:18.490
must be counted too.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.706
If the inner loop started at i + 1

00:02:21.778 --> 00:02:23.497
a subarray of length 1

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:25.074
would never be tried

00:02:25.074 --> 00:02:27.366
and on this all-negative array

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:29.301
whose answer is the single

00:02:29.373 --> 00:02:30.806
element -3

00:02:30.806 --> 00:02:33.600
the program would give a wrong answer.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.917
Can we beat n²?

00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:38.917
Follow the divide-and-conquer recipe.

00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:41.285
One element is the base case.

00:02:41.285 --> 00:02:42.154
Otherwise

00:02:42.154 --> 00:02:45.312
divide the array at the middle index k

00:02:45.312 --> 00:02:46.180
here 4.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.142
A maximum subarray lies in exactly

00:02:49.218 --> 00:02:50.755
one of three places.

00:02:50.755 --> 00:02:52.828
Entirely in the left half

00:02:52.828 --> 00:02:55.593
that is the left half's own answer

00:02:55.593 --> 00:02:56.976
a recursive call.

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.127
Entirely in the right half

00:02:59.127 --> 00:03:00.970
another recursive call.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.230
Or it crosses the middle

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.668
starting at or before k and ending after it.

00:03:06.744 --> 00:03:08.731
That case is not a smaller

00:03:08.807 --> 00:03:10.335
copy of the problem

00:03:10.412 --> 00:03:12.551
the crossing subarray is not

00:03:12.627 --> 00:03:14.690
the answer for either half.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.600
It needs its own method.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.837
A crossing subarray has two parts

00:03:19.837 --> 00:03:21.400
one that ends at k

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.135
and one that starts at k + 1.

00:03:24.135 --> 00:03:27.496
The two parts can be chosen independently.

00:03:27.496 --> 00:03:30.465
So take the best part that ends at k

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.341
plus the best part that

00:03:32.419 --> 00:03:34.060
starts at k + 1.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.494
Before we walk: what is the best crossing

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.880
subarray of this array, and what is its sum?

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.610
Pause and work it out.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.121
Walk left from k

00:03:48.121 --> 00:03:53.004
keeping a running sum and the best so far.

00:03:53.004 --> 00:03:57.319
At 4: -5, best -5.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:02.430
At 3, add 17: 12, a new best.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.950
At 2, add -9: 3.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.523
At 1: 10.

00:04:07.523 --> 00:04:09.286
At 0: 7.

00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:14.246
So the best left part is a[3..4]

00:04:14.246 --> 00:04:16.120
with sum 12.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:20.005
Now walk right from k + 1.

00:04:20.005 --> 00:04:23.874
At 5: 28, the best.

00:04:23.874 --> 00:04:25.633
At 6: 7.

00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:28.330
At 7: 25.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.417
At 8: 19.

00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:32.640
At 9: 23.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.700
The best right part is just 28.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.585
Together: indices 3 to 5, 17

00:04:40.585 --> 00:04:42.782
-5, 28.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.741
12 + 28 is 40

00:04:45.741 --> 00:04:47.346
the answer we saw.

00:04:47.346 --> 00:04:51.150
Every element was visited once: linear time.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.877
Now the recursion puts it together.

00:04:53.938 --> 00:04:55.717
The two recursive calls solve

00:04:55.778 --> 00:04:57.250
the halves on their own.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.765
The left half's best is 17 alone

00:04:59.826 --> 00:05:01.359
and the right half's best

00:05:01.421 --> 00:05:02.770
is 28 alone.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.572
The crossing subarray, with 40

00:05:05.572 --> 00:05:06.437
beats both

00:05:06.437 --> 00:05:09.321
so the combine step returns case three.

00:05:09.394 --> 00:05:11.124
Notice that neither half

00:05:11.196 --> 00:05:13.720
could have found it: it needs both.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:14.916
In Java

00:05:14.916 --> 00:05:18.225
maxCrossSubarray does exactly this walk.

00:05:18.225 --> 00:05:20.996
leftSum starts at the smallest int

00:05:20.996 --> 00:05:23.228
standing for minus infinity

00:05:23.228 --> 00:05:25.460
and the running sum at zero.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:29.407
p goes from k down to i, adding a[p].

00:05:29.407 --> 00:05:32.358
Whenever the sum beats leftSum

00:05:32.358 --> 00:05:35.770
it records the sum and the position.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.186
The second loop is the mirror image

00:05:39.186 --> 00:05:41.912
q goes from k + 1 up to j.

00:05:41.997 --> 00:05:44.041
It returns both ends and

00:05:44.126 --> 00:05:46.000
leftSum + rightSum.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.648
And the whole algorithm.

00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:52.180
A range of one element is the base case.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.205
Divide at k = (i + j) / 2.

00:05:55.205 --> 00:05:58.312
Conquer the two halves recursively

00:05:58.312 --> 00:06:01.850
and find the crossing subarray directly.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:02.928
Combine

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:06.930
if the left answer is at least both others

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:07.931
return it.

00:06:07.931 --> 00:06:11.296
Else if the right one is, return it.

00:06:11.296 --> 00:06:14.480
Otherwise return the crossing one.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:17.511
The tests use greater than or equal

00:06:17.511 --> 00:06:19.894
so in a tie the left case wins.

00:06:19.969 --> 00:06:22.054
Any of the tied answers is a

00:06:22.128 --> 00:06:23.990
correct maximum subarray.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.592
The demo runs it on our three examples

00:06:27.592 --> 00:06:30.613
and finds 118, 40

00:06:30.613 --> 00:06:31.930
and -3.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:36.990
A fourth array gives 4, -1, 2

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.120
1, with sum 6.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:40.794
For every array

00:06:40.794 --> 00:06:44.350
the brute force and the prefix sums agree.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.490
So it is correct on these inputs.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.180
But how fast is it?

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.351
Let T(n) be the worst-case

00:06:52.444 --> 00:06:56.237
cost on n elements, and add up the lines.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.921
The base case and the divide

00:06:59.013 --> 00:07:00.679
step are constant.

00:07:00.679 --> 00:07:04.472
Conquering costs T(n/2)

00:07:04.472 --> 00:07:05.120
twice.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:07.550
The crossing subarray walks

00:07:07.628 --> 00:07:10.941
the whole range once, so it is linear in n.

00:07:11.018 --> 00:07:13.252
Picking the best of the three

00:07:13.329 --> 00:07:14.870
answers is constant.

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.571
So T(n) is 2T(n/2)

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:22.634
plus O(n): the merge-sort recurrence.

00:07:22.634 --> 00:07:25.680
a is 2, b is 2, d is 1

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.829
and 2 equals 2 to the 1

00:07:28.829 --> 00:07:32.078
case one of the master theorem

00:07:32.078 --> 00:07:33.500
O(n log n).

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.256
The recursion tree says the same

00:07:36.256 --> 00:07:37.788
log₂n levels

00:07:37.788 --> 00:07:40.850
and each level scans n elements in total.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:45.303
What do the three bounds mean in practice?

00:07:45.303 --> 00:07:48.531
This program counts the additions.

00:07:48.531 --> 00:07:52.036
For 10 elements: 220

00:07:52.036 --> 00:07:54.710
65, and 34.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.974
For 10,000 elements

00:07:57.047 --> 00:08:00.064
divide and conquer does about

00:08:00.138 --> 00:08:02.051
130,000 additions

00:08:02.051 --> 00:08:04.333
the prefix sums 50 million

00:08:04.406 --> 00:08:06.540
and the brute force about

00:08:06.614 --> 00:08:08.380
170 billion.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:12.131
There is even a linear algorithm, Kadane's

00:08:12.131 --> 00:08:13.634
in exercise 8.

00:08:13.713 --> 00:08:16.797
But the divide-and-conquer idea and its

00:08:16.877 --> 00:08:19.566
analysis carry over to merge sort

00:08:19.566 --> 00:08:22.650
quicksort and many algorithms to come.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:25.015
To sum up the lecture.

00:08:25.015 --> 00:08:28.179
One: the cost of a recursive algorithm

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.558
is a recurrence

00:08:29.558 --> 00:08:31.992
and we solve it by unrolling

00:08:31.992 --> 00:08:33.858
with a recursion tree

00:08:33.939 --> 00:08:36.049
or by guessing and proving

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.590
with substitution.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:40.637
Two: the master theorem.

00:08:40.637 --> 00:08:45.018
aT(n/b) + O(n^d)

00:08:45.018 --> 00:08:48.726
is n^d log n, n^d

00:08:48.726 --> 00:08:53.444
or n^(log_b a), as a equals

00:08:53.444 --> 00:08:58.500
is less than, or is greater than b^d.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.421
Three: maximum subarray takes

00:09:01.504 --> 00:09:03.246
n³ by brute force

00:09:03.246 --> 00:09:05.568
n² with prefix sums

00:09:05.568 --> 00:09:08.471
and n log n by divide and conquer

00:09:08.554 --> 00:09:11.457
because the crossing case is solved

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:13.530
directly in linear time.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:15.675
Try this yourself

00:09:15.675 --> 00:09:19.563
trace maxSubarray on 2, -4

00:09:19.563 --> 00:09:21.358
3, -1.

00:09:21.358 --> 00:09:25.446
List the calls in the order they return

00:09:25.446 --> 00:09:27.440
with their results.

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:30.230
Next lecture: lists.

